r students and families are  still facing um because despite having  done that work and you know there's a  lot of cities nationally who have liked  stories um  and i think that's you know that that in  itself is progress but we still have  students that aren't able to participate  in school on a daily basis  um and we're you know we've launched a  number of things to address that but  certainly have still  more room to grow  um  and so i would describe that uh  overarchingly as sort of like what's  happened in detroit um and for me you  know one of the things i in my role as  assistant superintendent for curriculum  instruction you know teaching and  learning is at the core the heart of  what i do um and so  we have been really really  focused on making sure that our students  have continued access and our teachers  have continued access to high quality  instructional materials um that are  highly rated that are um  you know  part of our work and that's part of what  we're really still pursuing um is  culturally relevant materials  um and so that's a point of pride but  there are still nuances like there's  multiple platforms and that's hard for  kids and hard for families so  even as i share celebrations um each of  those those um accomplishments are  coupled with  um things that we're still working on to  improve um what we're doing for our  students  and you know there's there's a lot in  that and once again thank you for the  heroic effort that you and your fellow  district leaders have been doing  you know i'm curious about that  percentage of kids that or whatever the  percentage is that that haven't been  able to engage you know how large has  that been we've been hearing that from a  lot of districts you know i live in a  middle income upper middle income  suburban small school district a school  teacher shares the cul-de-sac with me  she was telling me that 5-10 of her kids  haven't been engaged i could imagine the  problem could be worse in detroit how  big is the problem there and what are  the best explanations for why these kids  haven't been able to engage in what are  you all trying uh to keep them engaged  yeah great question um you know we've  really been looking at uh student  engagement and attendance from a number  of different  uh perspectives um we you know our  enrollment actually as a district has  continued to increase  um and we were making really significant  progress on uh both reducing the number  of chronically absent students  increasing enrollment and increasing  just healthy daily attendance rates  district-wide with some exemplar schools  but the pandemic has really  impacted and our data looks similar to  what it did about two years ago  um students are you know students are  it's not consistent as  you know we don't have students who are  we do have some students who are not  coming at all but  it's inconsistent in terms of period of  the day or day of the week  um and the way that we're really so  we're thinking about that in two ways  the first um is really intentional  outreach um and so our we've leveraged  all school staff  um to reach out to families to do well  checks to offer  um supports beyond um just academic  support so we're thinking about the  mental health of the family or what  additional needs they they may have  that is food insecurity or utility  assistance um in our school culture  facilitators and our deans and our  school staff it's all hands on deck  trying to reach out to families to  understand why their students may not be  participating  beyond that  we have a team of district team members  who led a large effort including  district volunteers  and others to canvas and to really go to  every home  for our students who haven't  showed up um and you know that was work  i actually did myself in our  neighborhoods meeting families and  talking to them face to face of course  outside socially distance safely with  masks on  about what challenges they're facing  um and 