hings and honestly they're things as a  professional i use also  um and i think some of our students have  enjoyed a little bit  of autonomy  um and so  you know i i will say there are positive  things that will have long-term  implications for what in-person learning  looks like um because i think kids will  find just writing a notebook paper  potentially archaic  now that they have their own device so  um and there are still there are  struggles uh for educators and for  teachers in this but we actually as  educators i'm really glad that question  was asked because we do need you know  we're so focused on like what do we got  to do better but we do need to also  reflect on what kinds of things have  have benefited students and i'll name  one last thing as a benefit and that is  uh expanded equitable access so we've  actually been able to expand students  access to dual enrollment in the  district with our university partners  because it wasn't happening in person so  we weren't limited to just one school so  we have students participating across  schools  and so we've been able to serve more  students  and so i think that also has  implications district-wide for when we  return you know how do we make sure  every student in the district who wants  to take  calc 3  can  uh with a university professor  no that's great well  you know these conversations always go  by faster than i expect uh you know  thank you so much uh for joining us and  you know frankly thank you for the work  that you and all the other educators not  just in detroit but around the world are  doing right now to keep kids learning uh  but it shows that you know y'all are  doing  i would say better than than what what  we could hope for given how strains and  circumstances are and i you know there's  a lot to worry about uh with these kids  who seem to be kind of falling off and  failing or not engaged but there's some  there's many things to be hopeful for  and it's because of your tireless effort