ig is the problem there and what are  the best explanations for why these kids  haven't been able to engage in what are  you all trying uh to keep them engaged  yeah great question um you know we've  really been looking at uh student  engagement and attendance from a number  of different  uh perspectives um we you know our  enrollment actually as a district has  continued to increase  um and we were making really significant  progress on uh both reducing the number  of chronically absent students  increasing enrollment and increasing  just healthy daily attendance rates  district-wide with some exemplar schools  but the pandemic has really  impacted and our data looks similar to  what it did about two years ago  um students are you know students are  it's not consistent as  you know we don't have students who are  we do have some students who are not  coming at all but  it's inconsistent in terms of period of  the day or day of the week  um and the way that we're really so  we're thinking about that in two ways  the first um is really intentional  outreach um and so our we've leveraged  all school staff  um to reach out to families to do well  checks to offer  um supports beyond um just academic  support so we're thinking about the  mental health of the family or what  additional needs they they may have  that is food insecurity or utility  assistance um in our school culture  facilitators and our deans and our  school staff it's all hands on deck  trying to reach out to families to  understand why their students may not be  participating  beyond that  we have a team of district team members  who led a large effort including  district volunteers  and others to canvas and to really go to  every home  for our students who haven't  showed up um and you know that was work  i actually did myself in our  neighborhoods meeting families and  talking to them face to face of course  outside socially distance safely with  masks on  about what challenges they're facing  um and 