 math educator  and um so much of our work leading up  into this pandemic  you know with this with the current  student performance in the district you  know we have a number of students  already um who would be considered one  or more years behind  and so a really a huge part of our work  is providing tiered interventions to  those students and making sure that they  have the academic time and space to do  that work beyond just continuing with  high quality grade level instruction  um and so  you know we do have a foundation around  what you specifically spoke to which is  course recovery  we  we've done work over the last couple  years to have strong course recovery  structures in place  had um you know over the last summer we  had students recover about 600 courses  um and you know that's something that  will will build upon  as we move forward i think our job right  now given where we are in the school  year is to think about and enact  strategies to prevent some of that with  our educators so that that work can be  done before  students find themselves  in that space  and so not only are we doing work for  success with grade level instruction but  we're also actually expanding and  pursuing you know with this unique  structure with differentiated schedules  with things being online there are  actually also opportunities um to  provide direct services and  interventions to students in a way that  um  you know we can do in person but the  online structures have some value so  in fact in our high school we have  students um  who really need reading interventions i  mean i i realize at that point you know  most students are you know using reading  as a tool to learn and achieve in their  core courses but we've made a deep  commitment to addressing students  learning gaps so  uh we're launching and we've done work  um  our district-wide in fact our goal is to  reach over 9 000 students in tier 3  reading interventions um using this  opportunity and being able to fle